From: Radeon24 <Radeon24@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: fsoc2: Build without sibling calls on the Faraday
 FA726TE

On the FortiGate 60D, the Faraday FA726TE core of the FortiSOC2 has
been seen to occasionally skip an unconditional branch: instead of
jumping to its target, execution goes on with the next instruction in
memory. The case that was caught is the tail call that ends
pool_mayday_timeout():

	msr	CPSR_c, r6		@ local_irq_restore()
	...
	b	mod_timer

From time to time the branch is not taken and the CPU falls into
init_worker_pool(), laid out right after it, with r0 still pointing to
&pool->mayday_timer. That function then reinitialises the live worker
pool around it, which shows up a few seconds later as an oops in
create_worker() or in the worker pool code, on a fraction of the
boots, depending on the layout, and within a second when the mayday timer
is made to fire continuously.

The fault depends on where the branch sits: moving it by one word (a
nop before it) or replacing the tail call with a call and a return
both make it disappear, over thousands of timer runs. No such
behaviour of the core is documented, so it is described here as an
observed erratum; the exact trigger (instruction fetch around the
branch, interrupt return) is not known.

A function in which the branch is missed falls through into whatever
follows it, so every tail call emitted by gcc is a latent instance.
Add CPU_FA726TE_NO_SIBLING_CALLS, selected by ARCH_FSOC2, which builds
the C code with -fno-optimize-sibling-calls, the flag FRAME_POINTER
already imposes. A kernel built so has run the timer above more than
8 000 times without a fault. Text grows by about 0.35%.

Hand-written assembly keeps its own tail branches, and gcc may still
emit a branch for an identical-code-folding thunk; neither has been
seen to fault. Other platforms and the FortiSOC1 (FA626TE, where the
fault has not been seen) are unaffected.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Radeon24 <Radeon24@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 arch/arm/Kconfig.platforms |  1 +
 arch/arm/Makefile          |  5 +++++
 arch/arm/mm/Kconfig        | 12 ++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 18 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm/Kconfig.platforms
+++ b/arch/arm/Kconfig.platforms
@@ -109,6 +109,7 @@ config ARCH_FSOC2
 	depends on ARCH_MULTI_V5
 	depends on CPU_LITTLE_ENDIAN
 	select CPU_ARM926T
+	select CPU_FA726TE_NO_SIBLING_CALLS
 	select FARADAY_FTINTC010
 	select FTTMR010_TIMER
 	help
--- a/arch/arm/Makefile
+++ b/arch/arm/Makefile
@@ -152,6 +152,11 @@ KBUILD_CFLAGS	+=$(CFLAGS_ABI) $(CFLAGS_I
 KBUILD_AFLAGS	+=$(CFLAGS_ABI) $(AFLAGS_ISA) -Wa,$(arch-y) $(tune-y) -include $(srctree)/arch/arm/include/asm/unified.h -msoft-float
 KBUILD_RUSTFLAGS += --target=arm-unknown-linux-gnueabi
 
+# Faraday FA726TE: a missed tail call branch runs the next function instead
+ifeq ($(CONFIG_CPU_FA726TE_NO_SIBLING_CALLS),y)
+KBUILD_CFLAGS	+= -fno-optimize-sibling-calls
+endif
+
 CHECKFLAGS	+= -D__arm__
 
 # Text offset. This list is sorted numerically by address in order to
--- a/arch/arm/mm/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/arm/mm/Kconfig
@@ -153,6 +153,18 @@ config CPU_ARM926T
 	  Say Y if you want support for the ARM926T processor.
 	  Otherwise, say N.
 
+config CPU_FA726TE_NO_SIBLING_CALLS
+	bool
+	help
+	  The Faraday FA726TE has been seen to occasionally fall through an
+	  unconditional branch instead of taking it: execution goes on with
+	  the next instruction in memory. gcc turns calls in tail position
+	  into such a branch, so that a missed tail call silently runs the
+	  function laid out next with the caller's arguments.
+
+	  Selected by the platforms built around this core, to build the C
+	  code with -fno-optimize-sibling-calls.
+
 # FA526
 config CPU_FA526
 	bool
